---
import PublicationLayout from '../../layouts/PublicationLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'current-status', label: 'Current status' },
  { id: 'observation', label: 'What we observed' },
  { id: 'scope', label: 'Scope' },
  { id: 'public-evidence', label: 'Public evidence' },
  { id: 'preprint-relation', label: 'Relation to the preprint' },
];
---

<PublicationLayout
  category="Engineering"
  type="Bug report"
  title="An extreme-tail sign error in SciPy’s Student-t quantile"
  description="A SciPy bug can return positive infinity instead of a large negative value for an extreme-tail Student-t quantile."
  published="August 23, 2026"
  status="Fix merged in Boost.Math — not yet released"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="current-status">
    <h2>Current status</h2>
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        Reported to SciPy on August 20, 2026. The SciPy issue remains open and is labeled
        <code>defect</code>, <code>scipy.special</code>, and <code>upstream bug</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A SciPy contributor requested an upstream Boost report. We opened Boost.Math
        issue #1436 on August 24, 2026 and cross-linked it from SciPy. A Boost.Math
        repository collaborator reproduced the failure directly in Boost.Math and then
        opened pull request #1437 to address it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Boost.Math has now merged #1437 and closed #1436 as completed. The fix prevents
        underflow in the Student-t inverse correction calculation where possible and adds
        regression tests for very small quantile probabilities across several degrees of
        freedom. The correction is merged on Boost.Math’s <code>develop</code> branch; we
        have not confirmed it in a released Boost.Math or SciPy version yet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="observation">
    <h2>What we observed</h2>
    <p>
      For very small tail probabilities, <code>scipy.special.stdtrit</code> and
      <code>scipy.stats.t.ppf</code> can return <code>+inf</code> (positive infinity) even
      though the correct Student-t quantile is a large, negative, finite number. The
      report includes an input that reproduces the error and an independent
      high-precision calculation of the expected value.
    </p>
    <p>
      For one reported case with three degrees of freedom, the returned value is
      <code>+inf</code>, while the expected result is approximately
      <code>-2.31e82</code>. This is not a small rounding difference: the returned result
      has the wrong sign and incorrectly reports infinity.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="scope">
    <h2>Scope</h2>
    <p>
      The observed failures occur at exceptionally small probabilities, with onset that
      depends on the degrees of freedom. This report does not claim that ordinary
      Student-t analyses are affected, nor does it estimate how often real research
      workflows reach the failing region.
    </p>
    <p>
      The direct Boost.Math reproduction showed that the failure was present in the
      Boost.Math Student-t quantile path rather than only in SciPy’s Python-facing layer.
      Boost.Math’s merged fix restructures the derivative calculation used by the final
      correction step to avoid intermediate underflow where possible, and stops that
      correction when the derivative itself has underflowed. The pull request also adds
      regression tests covering extreme probabilities for multiple degrees of freedom.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page distinguishes a merged upstream correction from a released fix. Boost.Math
      #1437 is merged, but the latest Boost.Math release predates that merge, and the SciPy
      issue remains open. We will update the status again when an installable release that
      contains the correction is confirmed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="public-evidence">
    <h2>Public evidence</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/scipy/scipy/issues/25968" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SciPy issue #25968</a>
        — report, reproducer, labels, and current state
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/scipy/scipy/issues/25968#issuecomment-5374411963" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SciPy contributor response</a>
        — request to report the issue upstream to Boost
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/scipy/scipy/issues/25968#issuecomment-5391429348" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SciPy follow-up</a>
        — cross-link to the upstream Boost.Math report
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/boostorg/math/issues/1436" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Boost.Math issue #1436</a>
        — upstream report, direct reproduction, and completed close
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/boostorg/math/issues/1436#issuecomment-5393925442" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Boost.Math reproduction</a>
        — direct C++ reproducer and initial source-location diagnosis
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/boostorg/math/pull/1437" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Boost.Math pull request #1437</a>
        — underflow fix, regression tests, and merged correction
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Boost.Math has accepted and merged a correction for the upstream defect. We will
      continue to distinguish that merged state from release availability and will update
      this page when the correction is confirmed in a released Boost.Math or SciPy version.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="preprint-relation">
    <h2>Relation to the preprint</h2>
    <p>
      We found this issue while testing Student-t calculations for the
      <a href="/research/correctly-rounded-or-refused/">Correctly Rounded or Refused</a>
      preprint. This page tracks the software bug and its status. It is not peer review of
      the paper and does not show that any scientific analysis is valid.
    </p>
  </section>
</PublicationLayout>
